Day 70 - Implement Queue using Stacks

Problem Statement:
Implement a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) queue using only two stacks.
The queue should support push, pop, peek, and empty operations.

Code:
class MyQueue {
public:
    stack<int> s1;
    stack<int> s2;

    MyQueue() {
        
    }
    
    void push(int x) {
        while(!s1.empty()) {
            s2.push(s1.top());
            s1.pop();
        }

        s1.push(x);

        while(!s2.empty()) {
            s1.push(s2.top());
            s2.pop();
        }
    }
    
    int pop() {
        int ans = s1.top();
        s1.pop();
        return ans;
    }
    
    int peek() {
        return s1.top();
    }
    
    bool empty() {
        return s1.empty();
    }
};





Time Complexity:
push() → O(n)
pop() → O(1)
peek() → O(1)
empty() → O(1)

Space Complexity:
O(n)

The two stacks together can store up to n elements.

Approach:
This solution uses two stacks and rearranges the elements during push() so that the oldest element is always at the top of s1.

For push(x):
Move all elements from s1 to s2.
Push the new element x into s1.
Move all elements from s2 back to s1.
Now the oldest element is always at the top of s1.

For pop():
Remove and return the top element of s1.

For peek():
Return the top element of s1 without removing it.

For empty():
Check whether s1 is empty.
